  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/9/9f/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_99.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A mysterious, \"repulsive\" figure watches Thor fly off from a New York City street and thinks to himself that it's time to pay a visit to Dr. Don Blake -- \"an <i>unexpected</i> visit from -- <i>Mister Hyde!</i>\"\n\nMeanwhile, in Asgard, Thor again pleads with Odin to let him marry the mortal Jane Foster, but Odin is having none of it!&nbsp; Thor points out that Odin can make a human being an immortal, and Odin is shocked he would even suggest that!&nbsp; Thor is so angry at being dismissed, that he raises his hammer as if to strike his own father, an action he immediately regrets.&nbsp; Odin retaliates, yet seems to understand the sorrow this rule of Asgard is having on his son, and so he leaves Thor with the hope that, perhaps Jane will one day <i>be worthy of immortality</i>.&nbsp; And when that day comes, he may again come and present his petition.&nbsp; Thor is heartened that the door hasn't been permanently slammed shut, thanks his father, and joyously returns to Earth, specifically to the office of Don Blake.\n\nBut all the while he has been gone, the mysterious figure called Mr. Hyde strolls through the city streets, on his way to Don Blake's office.&nbsp; As he walks, we learn that Blake owes him \"an old debt,\" one that he's coming to collect!\n\nHe pauses outside the office building to reflect that he's been transformed, his strength \"magnified a dozen times.\"&nbsp; No, Blake will have no way of knowing that he was once Calvin Zabo, who approached the doctor months ago, looking for a job.&nbsp; However, Blake had heard of his poor reputation and how he had been \"fired from every job you've ever held!\"&nbsp; Sent away, Zabo exited from the interview, bitter about the experience, and vowed at that point to get <i>vengeance</i> on Don Blake.\n\nFascinated for years about the story of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde, Zabo returned home, developed a potion, which indeed turned him into Mr. Hyde, a man with the strength of twelve men!&nbsp; But he also started to hate humanity and began to commit crimes as Mr. Hyde, escaping detection by reverting back to Calvin Zabo.&nbsp; Now he's coming to get revenge on Don Blake.\n\nHyde bursts into Blake's outer office at the same time that Thor returns to the <i>inner </i>one and changes back to the mortal Don Blake.&nbsp; In the next moment, with a [SOUNDEFFECT: text=CRRASSSH!], Hyde storms into the inner office and (perhaps a bit unwittingly) hurls the doctor out the window as Jane Foster watches.&nbsp; Seeing the man she loves sent flying to his doom, Jane Foster faints, leaving Mr. Hyde free to rob Blake's files and research.\n\nDon Blake strikes his cane against the side of the building as he falls, and becomes Thor, temporarily returning to Blake's office to revive Jane and assure her that Dr. Blake is okay.&nbsp; But for now, he has to go out to track down this Mr. Hyde.&nbsp; Later, Hyde learns of Thor \"rescuing\" Blake, and now he's mad at Thor for ruining his revenge on Blake, and swears to destroy him!\n\nBut the next day, at a savings and loan, Thor shows up and, to the astonishment of all present, actually begins to <i>rob</i> it!&nbsp; It can't <b><i>be</i></b>!&nbsp; Thor has turned evil!&nbsp; The news of his apparent turn to crime causes the police to initiate a manhunt for the Thunder God.&nbsp; Radio broadcasts and newspaper headlines cause the public to panic, and ordinary citizens remain at home \"behind locked doors.\"\n\nNew York City \"takes on the aspect of a town under siege!\"&nbsp; The specter of an evil Thor paralyzes the city!\n\n[CONTINUED: text=Don't dare miss the upcoming 100<sup>th</sup> issue!]",
  "grade_one_liner": "A tale split between mythic weight and pedestrian villainy, with the former doing most of the heavy lifting.&nbsp; <b>Conclusion</b>:&nbsp; \"Well... <i>parts</i> of it were good.\"",
  "subtitle": "Hyde Your Eyes -- And Don't Look Too Closely at the Plot",
  "review_text": "A story that mainly focuses its attention on the villain-of-the-month motif, yet provides a couple of pages of Asgardian intrigue.\n\n[ASSET: left | WeakOrigin | width=17%] Focusing on the former first, the whole backstory of Calvin Zabo and his plan to get a job with the lame doctor to \"rob him later, at my leisure\" is incredibly weak.\n[BR]\n[BR] Has there <i>ever</i> been so much as a <i>hint</i> that Dr. Don Blake needed additional help in his medical practice?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=I think not!]\n\nHowever, for the sake of leniency, I'm willing to grant the possibility that the \"want ad\" Zabo was responding to might have been sent out during the brief time Jane Foster left to work for Dr. Andrews in last month's issue.&nbsp; (But note also that Jane Foster was <i>present</i> during Zabo's interview, so he got there too late to find the opening still available -- and that's not even to <i>mention</i> Zabo's already bad reputation.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFor some reason, this seems to be Zabo's \"last straw,\" and his experiments with the Jekyll and Hyde potion (inspired by the 1886 Robert Louis Stevenson story) leads him to become the new Mr. Hyde.\n[ASSET: center | MrHydeIsBorn | width=67%]\n\nThe mechanics of the origin aside (it only shows that Stan Lee was well-read in English literature), the fact that Zabo and his new, [SPAN: severity=error; text=evil] alter-ego thought it necessary to interrupt his crime spree, to invade Blake's office and rob him of his \"money and papers\" is simply asking too much of the reader, and I thus question his motivation (even if you excuse the derivative origin story).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Why bother with Dr. Blake when you now have the means to rob entire banks?]\n[BR] So he refused you a <b>job</b> -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=big, friggin' deal!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Oh <i>boo hoo</i>!)]\n\nAnyone with a reputation bad enough for a potential employer to have heard about shouldn't be surprised when they are turned down!&nbsp; If Zabo had <i>this</i> poor of a reputation (being \"fired from every job you've ever had\"), he simply <i>must</i> have heard this from more than just Dr. Blake.&nbsp; Why fixate on <i>him</i>, of all people?\n\nIt's not as though Blake's tiny medical practice represents some grand obstacle to Hyde's ambitions; it's barely a speed bump compared to the scale of crimes he's already capable of committing.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I'll <i>tell</i> you why -- because of <b>Comic Book Convenience 101</b>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | OutTheWindow | width=18%] [ASSET: right | CaneAgainstWall | width=12%] The whole \"vengeance on Dr. Don Blake\" aspect of the plot is suspect, and I consider it the weakest element of the entire issue.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Yet it is this very plot point that ultimately brings the hero and villain together.]\n[BR]\n[BR] But to be honest, I <i>did</i> like the Blake-falling-out-the-window sequence.\n[BR] It was at least visually dramatic... watching him strike his cane against the wall on the way down.\n\n[ASSET: left | FoiledMyRevenge | width=9%] [ASSET: right | CreepyOldHouse | width=12%] Naturally, Thor \"saves\" Blake, and Hyde hears about it on the radio, and now <b>Thor</b> is the one the villain must battle!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=And by the way, isn't it interesting how this villain <i>also</i> lives in a creepy-looking mansion?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This seems to be contractually required of <i>all</i> Marvel villains!)]\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<b>Lo and behold:</b>\n[HANG] * An [SPAN: severity=error; text=evil] Thor shows up and begins a crime spree.\n[HANG] * The police begin a manhunt for his capture.\n[HANG] * Citizens are afraid to go out of their homes.\n[HANG] * New York City \"takes on the aspect of a town under siege!\"\n\nAnd the whole story comes to a close with the [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=TO BE CONTINUED] ending, all in time for next month's 100<sup>th</sup> issue!\n[ASSET: center | ToBeContinued | width=67%]\n\nGranted, the abrupt ending was a [SPAN: severity=success; text=major <i>plus</i>] and follows closely on the heels of last month's <i>Tales to Astonish</i> 50 ([LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14]), featuring the same sort of not-quite-cliffhanger ending, in this case showing the specter of a malevolent Thor posing menacingly above the city skyline.\n\nJust like with [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14], the words \"TO BE CONTINUED\" are not used, but as <i>this</i> one was likely written about the same time as <i>that</i> issue, I believe it should share some of the credit for pushing forward the use of the multi-part story.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=But let's be real here...] is <i>any</i> reader fooled by this [SPAN: severity=error; text=evil] \"Thor,\" especially given that one page earlier showed an angry Mr. Hyde declaring that Thor would never suspect how he'll eventually defeat him?\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Come on, now!&nbsp; <b><i>Seriously?</i></b>]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIf Mr. Hyde's wobbly backstory and questionable motivations were the issue's low points, its [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=best] moments were the ones between Thor and Odin.&nbsp; The whole interchange only takes up two of the story's thirteen pages (and are interrupted by Mr. Hyde's backstory), but I found them to be far more interesting than the villain-of-the-month.\n\nThey are the only scenes that give Thor an emotional through-line, reminding the reader that his conflict with Odin is the <i>real</i> long‑term engine of the feature.\n\nNote that page 3 of <i>last</i> month's story had a two-panel interlude in Asgard, which featured Odin summoning Thor to Asgard, only to order that he put aside all thoughts of \"the human Jane Foster.\"&nbsp; In that case, Thor simply turned around and essentially said, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"<i>Uh</i>... that doesn't work for me!\"]&nbsp; Odin bit his tongue when Thor walked out on him.\n\nIn <i>this</i> issue, Thor approaches Odin to plead his case once again, even temporarily losing his head and raising his hammer in protest [SPAN: severity=warning; text=at his own <i>father</i>,] before calming down after Odin raises the possibility that Jane might one day be worthy of immortality.\n\nIt was good to see Stan keeping that plot line active, even while the main part of the issue focused on the far lamer Mr. Hyde.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe story's unimpressive villain and backstory drag the issue down while the Asgard discussion between Thor and Odin, together with the out-the-window sequence and abrupt ending help raise its merits at the same time.\n\nThough it <i>does</i> mean that next month's 100<sup>th</sup> issue likely features Mr. Hyde once more (not a great thing), it at least makes sense and gives readers something to look forward to.&nbsp; Of <i>course</i>, it's not the 100<sup>th</sup> issue featuring <i>Thor</i> (as his feature began in [LINK:1962:JIM_83_thor0|JIM #83|2026-04-01]), but... let's not quibble over small details...\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=It's still a milestone to celebrate.]\n\nAs for <i>this</i> month, the unevenness of the storytelling, the questionable villain, the cringe factor of \"female\" Jane Foster <b><i>fainting</i></b> at the sight of Blake falling to his death (see the October 1963 summary for more), and the out-of-left-field [SPAN: severity=error; text=evil Thor] robbing a Savings and Loan all conspire to drag the nicer elements of the story back down to Earth, keeping this one grounded at its final resting place.\n\nIt's an issue pulled in two directions -- mythic ambition on one side, pedestrian villainy on the other -- and the imbalance keeps it as [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"interesting... but only up to a point.\"]",
  "side_note": [
    "The backup tale \"Stroom's Strange Solution\" turns out to have the same \"solution\" as Reed Richards' fix for the dilemma his team faced in [LINK:1962:FF_7|FF #7|2026-04-01], published about 15 months earlier.&nbsp; Obviously, plotter Stan Lee was recycling an old plot, hoping no one would notice...",
    "<b>Tales of Asgard </b>introduces us to \"Surtur the Fire Demon!\" (done by Lee and Kirby as opposed to Lee and Heck, as in the main tale)\n\nOdin learns that the Trolls have joined Surtur to rebel against Asgard, and he personally invades their land!&nbsp; He bests the Trolls, and they tell him where to find Surtur.&nbsp; Odin has to venture into the \"sea of flame!\"&nbsp; Surtur's initial attack on Odin is unsuccessful, so he strikes Earth instead, boring deep into the planet and extracting a \"huge chunk of matter,\" which he sends into the sky around the Earth \"and will be forever known as -- the Moon!\"\n\nBack home, Odin creates the Rainbow Bridge for the first time, to help speed his way to Earth.&nbsp; Odin causes the Earth to rotate (something it is still doing), trapping Surtur \"in the center of the planet.\"&nbsp; Surtur sends Odin a flying horse, trying to get the Asgardian to free him.&nbsp; But he can be patient.&nbsp; \"He has all <i>eternity</i> to wait!\"\n\n(Obviously this tale is adapted from some actual Norse legends -- not the most inventive of tales, and one that doesn't make a lot of sense.)"
  ],
  "ownership": "I was lucky enough to obtain a FN/VF copy of this book as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $30, ultimately selling on eBay in November 2019 for $89.50, netting me over $48.\n[DEVNOTE: My other copy came from some other place, and I've elected to keep it.]",
  "highlight": "Thor and Mr. Hyde square off in a tale where the Asgard drama soars, Dr. Blake plummets, and the villain's entire motivations remain questionable."
}